    "summary": "Dynamic set with standout Other One and tight Good Lovin' jam",
    "blurb": "The concert opens with a sprightly acoustic half before exploding into an electric second set highlighted by a fiery The Other One and the quirky Cowboy Song. Jerry’s lead work shines on Good Lovin' and Not Fade Away, while the rhythm section stays locked tight throughout.",
    "review": "On April 9, 1970 at Fillmore West the Dead delivered a show that swings from folk‑rock intimacy to high‑octane improvisation. The first half leans heavily on acoustic favorites – Me And My Uncle, Casey Jones and a plaintive rendition of It’s A Man’s World – before the band launches into an electric surge. Highlights include a blistering double take on Good Lovin' that bookends a drum duet, a wild Cowboy Song* that some fans compare to a Marshall Tucker riff, and a soaring The Other One that rides a relentless groove into the second Cryptical Envelopment. Not Fade Away and Turn On Your Lovelight close the set with pure, foot‑stomping energy. While the matrix recording mixes a sublime acoustic board sound with uneven audience patches, it still captures the band’s tight interplay; the audience tape offers crystal‑clear 1970 capture for those who prefer pure live ambience.",
